WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.080
The past decade has left us with a good share
of movie mysteries.

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:08.760
From misplaced lightsabers to dreaming mercenaries,
here's what we think are the biggest unanswered

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.410
movie questions of the past decade.

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:16.000
In Blade Runner, Harrison Ford plays the character
of Deckard, a cop tasked with killing very

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.599
specific targets: synthetic life forms known
as replicants.

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.020
"They were designed to copy human beings in
every way, except their emotions.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:28.580
The designers reckon that after a few years,
they might develop their own emotional responses."

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.780
However, one of the biggest bones of contention
between fans and even people intimately involved

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:38.800
in making the film is the question of whether
or not its lead character is himself a replicant.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.360
In 2014, director Ridley Scott told Digital
Spy that without a doubt:

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.960
"He is definitely a replicant."

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.730
Harrison Ford, on the other hand, is on record
saying Deckard is a human, and writer Hampton

00:00:50.730 --> 00:00:51.960
Fancher agrees with him.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:57.380
Debating fans may have been hoping that the
film's 2017 sequel, Blade Runner 2049, would

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:00.240
clear things up, but if so, they were wrong.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:05.360
Similar to the original film, Blade Runner
2049 ends without a definitive answer regarding

00:01:05.440 --> 00:01:08.040
the question of Deckard's humanity or lack
thereof.

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:12.500
We learn that Deckard and the late replicant
Rachel somehow conceived a child, and Jared

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:17.080
Leto's character, Wallace, gives us a monologue
exploring the question, but we never learn

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.140
the truth either way.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:22.440
It seems likely this is one question that
audiences will have to accept will remain

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.200
unanswered for many years to come.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:29.340
Exactly how does Maz Kanata get her hands
on Anakin Skywalker's lightsaber?

00:01:30.080 --> 00:01:35.500
The diminutive alien offers the decades-old
weapon to Rey in her tavern on Takodana in

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.560
2015's Star Wars: The Force Awakens.

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:42.310
Freaked out by Force visions, Rey initially
refuses it, and as a result, Finn is briefly

00:01:42.310 --> 00:01:43.300
its caretaker.

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.500
However, Rey finally takes it up in order
to defeat Kylo Ren at the end of the film.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.120
But how did the lightsaber wind up in Takodana
in the first place?

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:55.340
In order of the Skywalker saga's narrative,
we first see Anakin Skywalker's lightsaber

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:57.520
in 2005's Revenge of the Sith.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.160
Anakin uses it before his transformation into
Darth Vader, and Obi-Wan Kenobi takes it from

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.360
Anakin when the latter is defeated at the
end of Revenge of the Sith.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:09.660
Kenobi gives Luke the lightsaber in 1977's
A New Hope.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.640
"Your father wanted you to have this when
you were old enough."

00:02:13.460 --> 00:02:17.700
The last time we see it before The Force Awakens
is in the climactic battle between Luke and

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.600
Vader on the planet Bespin at the end of 1980's
The Empire Strikes Back.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.999
The weapon is in Luke's hand when Vader chops
it off, sending it down the long shaft the

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:26.880
pair duel over.

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:31.400
So how exactly did the saber make its way
to a container in a tavern on a different

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.480
planet in a different solar system?

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:36.659
"A good question...for another time."

00:02:37.060 --> 00:02:40.820
Well, we really don't have a better answer
other than "the Force willed it."

00:02:41.829 --> 00:02:46.439
The ending of 2010's Inception is the kind
that's likely to be debated for years, and

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:47.940
even decades, to come.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:52.900
Directed by Christopher Nolan, the film boasts
a heavyweight cast including Joseph Gordon-Levitt,

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.020
Tom Hardy, and Ellen Page, among others.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:59.680
Leonardo DiCaprio plays its lead, Cobb, who's
the head of a crew of thieves specializing

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:01.040
in corporate espionage.

00:03:01.349 --> 00:03:05.620
Cobb and his team enter their victims' dreams,
steal information, and plant ideas into their

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:06.620
subconscious.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.540
"Mr. Saito, this…this isn't your typical
corporate espionage.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:16.180
You asked me for inception, I do hope you
understand the, the gravity of that request."

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.740
One of the dangers of their work is the risk
of being stuck in a dream without knowing

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:20.240
it.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:24.319
So Cobb and his colleagues each carry totems,
which are items that let them know whether

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:25.420
or not they're in a dream.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:27.260
Cobb carries a spinning top.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.319
Whenever he doubts whether or not he's in
a dream, he spins it, and if the top doesn't

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:33.480
stop spinning, he knows he's still asleep.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.200
Inception ends with Cobb testing his totem
after being reunited with his children, but

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.020
we never learn whether or not the top stops
spinning.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.900
Cobb spins the top on the table, then walks
away.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.740
The screen fades to black with the top still
spinning.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.060
Nolan later explained he ended the film this
way to demonstrate that, to Cobb, it didn't

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.480
matter whether or not he was dreaming.

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:56.040
During his 2015 Princeton commencement speech,
Nolan said:

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:59.900
"[Cobb] didn't really care anymore, and that
makes a statement: Perhaps, all levels of

00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:00.909
reality are valid."

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:06.680
When the elderly Steve Rogers gives Sam Wilson
his shield at the end of 2019's Avengers:

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.500
Endgame, it may seem simple as far as time
travel plot points go.

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:15.040
Captain America goes back in time to return
the Infinity Stones, and rather than return,

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:18.769
he decides to stay in the past and spend as
much of the rest of his life as he can with

00:04:18.769 --> 00:04:19.620
Peggy Carter.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.280
Then, having lived much of that life, Cap
returns to the spot where he was sent back

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.360
in time and hands his shield over to Sam.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:26.740
What's confusing about that?

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.340
Well, here's the thing.

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:32.900
When Steve Rogers goes back in time and stays
with Peggy Carter, he changes history, and

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.660
in doing so, he creates an alternate timeline.

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:39.440
While speaking to Entertainment Weekly shortly
after the release of Endgame, directors Joe

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:43.630
and Anthony Russo said that Cap's new life
with Peggy does not take place in the prime

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:44.860
MCU timeline.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:49.639
So if he lives in this new splintered reality,
how does he get back to the prime MCU reality

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:51.920
in order to hand the shield off to Sam Wilson?

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.520
The Russos have admitted they don't have an
answer.

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:56.250
Joe told Entertainment Weekly:

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:00.680
"The question then becomes, how is [Steve
Rogers] back in this reality to give the shield

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:02.200
away?...Interesting question, right?

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:03.480
Maybe there's a story there."

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:07.300
In a world like the MCU's, there's no shortage
of possibilities.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:11.780
Only time will tell if we ever get a definitive
explanation for Steve Rogers' return.

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:16.040
The Arthur Fleck of 2019's Joker lives in
a fantasy world.

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.680
Early in the film, Fleck imagines himself
in the audience of his favorite late night

00:05:19.700 --> 00:05:23.840
talk show, eventually being called up to the
stage by host Murray Franklin.

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:28.460
Later, we learn he's even imagined an entire
romantic relationship with his neighbor, Sophie,

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:30.700
who in reality, barely remembers his name.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:35.280
So when we learn that Arthur's time with Sophie
was all in his head, it's difficult to not

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:37.820
call most or all of the film's events into
question.

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.620
When he finally does show up on the Murray
Franklin Show, for example, even though the

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:45.680
host doesn't treat him as nicely as he does
in Fleck's earlier fantasy, in many ways his

00:05:45.680 --> 00:05:47.700
behavior seems just as nonsensical.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.100
He allows Arthur to say horrible things on
live TV.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.090
He doesn't pull the plug but instead argues
with Arthur until the failed comedian finally

00:05:55.090 --> 00:05:56.000
kills the host.

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:00.650
On one extreme end of the spectrum, it could
be that almost the entire story is just Fleck's

00:06:00.650 --> 00:06:01.140
imagination.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:04.080
At the end of the movie, Fleck is in a mental
institution.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:07.900
Earlier in the film, while Fleck talks to
his social worker, there's a brief flashback

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.840
of him slamming his head into a door in the
same institution.

00:06:11.240 --> 00:06:15.620
It could be those are the only "real" parts
of the movie, and everything else is his fantasy.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:19.880
Either way, it's one of the biggest unanswered
questions in Joker, and one we might never

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:20.889
get an answer to.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:26.320
The acclaimed TV series Breaking Bad ends
with Jesse Pinkman racing his way to freedom.

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:30.440
After being enslaved as a meth cook for a
bunch of white supremacists, he's finally

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:35.420
free, and the Netflix movie El Camino tells
us what happens to Jesse once he reaches civilization.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:39.760
After encountering some old friends, discovering
a secret stash of cash, and running across

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:44.750
some really bad dudes, Jesse finds Ed - the
same guy who relocated Walter White toward

00:06:44.750 --> 00:06:49.020
the end of Breaking Bad - and convinces the
older man to smuggle him all the way to Alaska.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:52.260
"I'm looking for a uh, Hoover Max.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.949
Pressure Max, Max Pressure, Hoover, or maybe
it's a Pro Max.

00:06:56.949 --> 00:06:58.740
You got like uh, like a filter?"

00:06:59.180 --> 00:07:03.880
Once they're up north, Jesse gives Ed a letter
addressed to Brock, the son of Andrea Cantillo.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.860
In one of the most soul-crushing moments of
Breaking Bad, Jesse is forced to watch as

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:11.460
the white supremacists kill Andrea as punishment
for him trying to escape.

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:15.840
As for Brock, he was left orphaned and without
a clue as to why his mother was shot.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.640
What does Jesse write to Brock?

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:20.420
Well, we never get a chance to read the letter.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:21.800
We only see the envelope.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:25.660
It's tough to imagine what Jesse could possibly
say to the eight-year-old.

00:07:25.660 --> 00:07:29.260
Maybe it's better to not know, or our hearts
might get broken all over again.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:34.919
Most post-apocalyptic films begin and end
with only a small portion of the Earth's population

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:37.680
left, and Snowpiercer is no different in that
respect.

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:41.740
In the film, all that's left of the human
race is confined to a train that protects

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:44.240
them from the fatal cold of a second Ice Age.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.900
What's different about Snowpiercer is that
by the end of the film, it isn't clear how

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:49.440
much of the human race is left.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:54.500
A cruel aristocracy rules the locomotive of
Snowpiercer, with the poorest survivors confined

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:55.160
to the tail.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:59.420
The heroic Curtis leads a revolt and pushes
his followers through the length of the train

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:01.140
to the engine caretaker, Wilford.

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:06.480
And when things start getting desperate, one
of Curtis' allies, Namgoong, sets off an explosive

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:10.659
that triggers an avalanche, derailing the
train and presumably killing most of the people

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:11.320
on board.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:12.540
"Nice."

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:24.500
In the film's final moments, Yona and Timmy
- a teenage girl and a young boy - emerge

00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:25.340
from the train wreck.

00:08:25.660 --> 00:08:28.420
The movie ends just as they spot a polar bear
nearby.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:33.000
The explosion and the crash it causes are
dramatic enough that we have to assume few

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:37.220
survive, but as far as the named characters
are concerned, we never know for sure.

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:39.860
We don't see the bodies of Curtis, Namgoong,
or Wilford.

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:43.920
If Yona and Timmy really are all that's left
at the end of Snowpiercer, it doesn't seem

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.290
like humanity has long to go before the end.

00:08:47.320 --> 00:08:50.920
The warrior Sif has been missing from the
MCU since her appearance in Thor: The Dark

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:51.420
World.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:55.460
She wasn't there for the battle with Hela,
she wasn't on the Asgardian refugee ship in

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.240
Avengers: Infinity War, and she wasn't with
the Asgardians in Avengers: Endgame.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:03.750
According to Jaime Alexander, she wasn't there
for Ragnarok because of a scheduling conflict

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.160
between the movie's shooting schedule and
that of her TV series, Blindspot.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:11.840
Sif isn't mentioned in the film, but Marvel
Studios president Kevin Feige gave a narrative

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:16.450
explanation connected to Loki's secret usurping
of the Asgardian throne at the end of Thor:

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:16.980
The Dark World.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:22.180
Just as we learn in Ragnarok that Loki had
Heimdall branded a traitor, Feige said Loki

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:26.500
would want Thor's closest friends as far away
from Asgard as possible so he found some reason

00:09:26.500 --> 00:09:27.530
to exile her.

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:28.020
He said:

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:29.700
"Sif was probably banished.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:30.800
She's off somewhere."

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:35.800
However, neither Feige nor anyone else has
bothered to specify what "somewhere" means.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:40.710
At the end of The Dark Knight Rises, the final
installment of director Christopher Nolan's

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:44.600
Dark Knight trilogy, Bruce Wayne fakes his
own death, but he doesn't seem content to

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.160
leave Gotham without a protector.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:50.500
So he leaves a parcel for police detective
John Blake - whose first name we learn is

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:53.900
"Robin" - that leads Blake to the Batcave
and all its secrets.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:57.600
At the same time, James Gordon discovers the
Bat Signal has been repaired.

00:09:57.910 --> 00:10:01.860
The assumption seems to be that with the Batcave's
arsenal at his disposal, Blake will take up

00:10:01.870 --> 00:10:06.350
the mantle of Batman to make sure Gotham still
has its protection against international assassins

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:07.660
and psychotic clowns.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:11.100
But will that happen?

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:12.900
More to the point, could that happen?

00:10:13.120 --> 00:10:17.200
Throughout The Dark Knight Rises, Blake proves
himself dedicated to the cause of justice,

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:19.660
which is likely why Wayne chose him as a successor.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.520
Still, he can be as dedicated as he likes
but it doesn't change the fact that he's missing

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.560
some key components to being Batman.

00:10:25.980 --> 00:10:29.990
He doesn't have the billions of dollars at
Wayne's disposal, as that's largely left to

00:10:29.990 --> 00:10:30.780
his butler, Alfred.

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.560
And he doesn't have someone like Alfred to
bandage his wounds or Lucius Fox to design

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:35.160
new tech.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:39.620
He doesn't have the dual identity of a billionaire
playboy to help hide is crime-fighting, and

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:43.600
perhaps more importantly, he doesn't have
the decade Wayne spent abroad training to

00:10:43.600 --> 00:10:44.720
avenge his parents.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:47.000
Could Blake still fill Wayne's shoes?

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:47.680
Maybe.

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:51.580
But it's hardly a certainty, and now that
the movies have moved on from this story,

00:10:51.580 --> 00:10:52.880
we'll never find out.

